WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
Dangling access occurs when code keeps a way to reach an object or state after it is no longer valid.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:13.560
The access might be a pointer, reference, iterator, span, string view, or cached address.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:20.860
The type can still look reasonable, and the code can still be readable, but the lifetime assumption behind it is broken.

4
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:37.060
Common hazards include returning references to local objects, storing pointers into buffers whose owners have been destroyed, keeping views longer than their source data, and using references into containers after a modification that reallocated storage.

5
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.200
Moved-from objects add another source of confusion.

6
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.540
They remain valid only according to their documented state, not according to whatever happened to be true in one test run.

7
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.820
Aliasing makes these issues harder.

8
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:56.760
If several pieces of code hold access to the same object, a mutation through one path can invalidate assumptions held by another.

9
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:02.220
A vector growth operation may invalidate iterators, pointers, and references.

10
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.800
An erase operation may change which elements remain valid.

11
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.020
A string modification may invalidate a previously saved pointer to its characters.

12
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.320
Long-lived aliases to unstable storage make review and testing more difficult.

13
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.880
Prevention starts with interface design.

14
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.220
Keep borrowed access short-lived.

15
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.200
Prefer returning values or owning handles when lifetime would otherwise be ambiguous.

16
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.980
Document invalidation behavior near mutating operations.

17
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.080
Review async callbacks, caches, and stored views carefully.

18
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:42.660
The safer design makes stale access difficult to create and easy to identify before it becomes a production defect.

19
00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:50.060
Tests should include the operations that change validity, not just the operation that later observes the stale access.

20
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:57.040
Exercise container growth, erase paths, moves, resets, shutdown, and callback cancellation.

21
00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:02.420
These are the moments when an alias that seemed harmless can become invalid.

22
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:09.960
If those transitions are important to the design, make them visible in names, contracts, and review checklists.
